judged.hybrid_suggestion:
Combine C3's mechanism with C6's choreography: a 'Shy Bear' skin on C3's sealed twin-cam core. Make the bear's paws chunky, crossed and FIXED, as part of the front shell at eye height (r ≥ 2 mm), so the paws are no longer a separate small part. The cam-driven rocker head rises behind them through a storyboard synthesized for this beat: hidden with eyes behind the paws, then peek with eyes just clearing the paws (a dwell, with a deeper detent bump as in C5), then duck, then BOO (full pop with a 16° lean), then hide. That keeps C6's emotional peek-a-boo, the best character hook in the set, while using C3's safety architecture. It has three bodies, all larger than 31.7 mm, no flexures, a sealed nip, and only Z-overlap or constant-gap edges, so nothing graspable is smaller than the cylinder. Apply C3's fixes as well:
- widen the follower arms to ≥7 mm and add rocker-to-body hard stops at both swing extremes, so kids pushing the head down load the frame, not the 3.8:1 cam lever;
- put the dial scallops only on the 1 mm proud lip and make the dial ≥Ø36;
- chamfer the head edges where they slide under the shell;
- give raised eyes on the head so the face reads in single colour.
Optionally borrow C5's larger rise by lengthening the rocker lever, so the BOO reveal is around 18 mm.
